::ENG2_U04_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Findings: Compost bins reduced cafeteria trash weight. Signs helped students sort scraps correctly. Which topic sentence is strongest?{
~A. Compost bins are gross and weird.#Incorrect. This is biased and informal.
=B. Compost bins may reduce cafeteria trash when students have clear sorting directions.#Correct. This previews the source-based explanation clearly.
~C. Signs have words.#Incorrect. This is too narrow.
~D. Cafeterias are places.#Incorrect. This is too general.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Research findings explain how pre-order forms led to fewer unwanted trays. Which structure best fits?{
~A. Description only#Incorrect. The findings explain how one action produced an effect.
~B. Fictional plot#Incorrect. This is not a narrative.
~C. Definition only#Incorrect. The findings are not just defining a term.
=D. Cause and effect#Correct. The paragraph should explain how the pre-order process affected waste.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence has the best academic voice?{
=A. The district report suggests that pre-order forms can reduce unwanted trays when students complete them consistently.#Correct. This is precise, measured, and source-based.
~B. This source thing is pretty good, I guess.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~C. The program totally fixed lunch forever.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated.
~D. Lunch forms are the best ever.#Incorrect. This is biased and informal.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which transition best adds a related finding?{
~A. However#Incorrect. This signals contrast.
~B. As a result#Incorrect. This signals effect.
=C. In addition#Correct. This transition adds another related finding.
~D. Instead#Incorrect. This signals replacement or contrast.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence should be removed from an academic expository paragraph?{
=A. Only lazy students forget lunch forms.#Correct. This sentence is biased and attacks students.
~B. Some students needed reminders during the first week.#Incorrect. This is relevant source information.
~C. The pilot reduced unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is relevant evidence.
~D. Staff prepared meals based on student selections.#Incorrect. This explains how the system worked.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which sentence uses research findings responsibly?{
~A. The findings prove the program was perfect.#Incorrect. This exaggerates beyond the evidence.
~B. The findings are about nothing.#Incorrect. They address food waste reduction.
~C. The findings should be ignored.#Incorrect. The findings are relevant.
=D. The findings suggest the program reduced waste when forms were completed and reminders were used.#Correct. This includes the result and condition without exaggeration.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which order is most logical for a paragraph about the lunch form findings?{
~A. Random opinion, unrelated fact, source title, joke#Incorrect. This is not expository organization.
=B. Topic sentence, result, explanation of how it worked, limitation, support that improved the routine#Correct. This order explains the findings logically.
~C. Limitation first, no topic sentence, unrelated ending#Incorrect. The paragraph needs a clear explanation.
~D. Conclusion only#Incorrect. A paragraph needs development.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Findings: Source A explains benefits. Source B explains a challenge. Source C explains a support that helped. Which organization fits best?{
~A. Alphabetical order only#Incorrect. Alphabetical order would not show the relationship between findings.
~B. Random order#Incorrect. Random order weakens clarity.
=C. Problem and solution or cause and effect#Correct. The paragraph can explain the challenge and how support improved the program.
~D. Definition only#Incorrect. The findings go beyond definition.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which word is most precise for academic voice in this sentence: "The reminders _____ student completion of the forms."{
~A. did stuff to#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. messed with#Incorrect. This is informal.
~C. was like#Incorrect. This is vague and not standard academic wording.
=D. improved#Correct. "Improved" precisely explains the effect.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best explains the relationship between findings?{
=A. The form reduced waste, while reminders helped students use the form consistently.#Correct. This explains how the findings connect.
~B. Forms and reminders are different things.#Incorrect. This is true but does not explain the research relationship.
~C. The sources exist in the lesson.#Incorrect. This does not explain findings.
~D. Lunch is lunch.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence is an effective concluding sentence for an expository paragraph?{
~A. That's why lunch forms are totally perfect.#Incorrect. This is informal and exaggerated.
~B. So yeah, forms.#Incorrect. This is too informal.
=C. Overall, the findings show that pre-order forms can reduce waste when the school also supports students with reminders.#Correct. This summarizes the explanation without exaggeration.
~D. Everyone who disagrees is wrong.#Incorrect. This is biased.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] A paragraph first explains the problem of unwanted trays, then explains pre-order forms as a way to reduce them. Which structure is being used?{
~A. Compare and contrast only#Incorrect. The paragraph is not mainly comparing two options.
=B. Problem and solution#Correct. It names a problem and explains a solution.
~C. Poetry structure#Incorrect. It is not a poem.
~D. Random order#Incorrect. The paragraph has a clear organization.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which revision improves academic voice? Original: "The garden thing is really cool for science."{
=A. A school garden can support science learning by providing observations of plant growth and soil conditions.#Correct. This revision is precise and evidence-based.
~B. The garden thing is super cool for science stuff.#Incorrect. This remains informal.
~C. Gardens are cool, cool, cool.#Incorrect. This is repetitive and informal.
~D. Science garden awesome.#Incorrect. This is not a complete academic sentence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source finding best supports a paragraph about garden journals?{
~A. The cafeteria used student meal choices.#Incorrect. This is about lunch forms.
~B. Bus routes ended too early.#Incorrect. This is about transportation.
~C. Tool prices changed.#Incorrect. This does not support garden journals.
=D. Journals help students record and compare observations over time.#Correct. This directly supports a paragraph about garden journals.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best integrates a limitation?{
~A. The program was perfect.#Incorrect. This ignores limitations.
~B. The program failed completely.#Incorrect. This ignores benefits.
=C. Although the pilot reduced unwanted trays, some students needed reminders during the first week.#Correct. This includes both a benefit and limitation.
~D. Reminders are annoying.#Incorrect. This is opinion-based.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which transition best signals contrast?{
~A. In addition#Incorrect. This adds information.
=B. However#Correct. "However" signals contrast.
~C. For example#Incorrect. This introduces an example.
~D. As a result#Incorrect. This signals effect.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which paragraph plan best fits school garden findings?{
~A. Joke, personal opinion, unrelated source, ending#Incorrect. This is not expository writing.
~B. List of random garden objects#Incorrect. This does not explain research findings.
=C. Topic sentence, observation finding, journal finding, vocabulary connection, concluding sentence#Correct. This plan logically explains how the findings support science learning.
~D. Conclusion with no evidence#Incorrect. Evidence is needed.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase best fits academic voice?{
=A. "may support"#Correct. This phrase is measured and appropriate for research-based writing.
~B. "super amazing"#Incorrect. This is informal and exaggerated.
~C. "kinda does stuff"#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. "totally fixes"#Incorrect. This is exaggerated.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Why should a writer choose structure before drafting?{
~A. Structure makes evidence unnecessary.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
=B. Structure helps the reader understand how findings relate to one another.#Correct. Organization makes relationships clear.
~C. Structure prevents transitions.#Incorrect. Structure supports transitions.
~D. Structure means the paragraph cannot have a topic sentence.#Incorrect. A paragraph still needs a topic sentence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence is too informal for expository writing?{
~A. The findings suggest that reminders improved form completion.#Incorrect. This is academic.
~B. Student reminders supported the routine during the first week.#Incorrect. This is academic.
~C. The pilot reduced unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is clear and evidence-based.
=D. The reminders were, like, a big help with the form thing.#Correct. This is informal and vague.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which topic sentence is strongest for a paragraph about later library hours?{
=A. Later library hours may increase access for students who cannot visit before 5:00 p.m.#Correct. This sentence is focused and evidence-based.
~B. Libraries are great and everyone loves them.#Incorrect. This is broad and biased.
~C. Books are in libraries.#Incorrect. This is too narrow.
~D. I like evening hours.#Incorrect. This is personal opinion.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which sentence misuses a research finding by exaggerating it?{
~A. The pilot reduced unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This states the finding.
~B. Students needed reminders at first.#Incorrect. This states a limitation.
=C. The pilot proved that all cafeteria waste everywhere is solved forever.#Correct. This exaggerates far beyond the source.
~D. Staff prepared meals from selections.#Incorrect. This explains how the pilot worked.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best connects evidence to explanation?{
~A. The report exists.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
~B. There are trays.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. Lunch is a time of day.#Incorrect. This does not connect evidence.
=D. Because staff prepared meals from student selections, fewer unwanted trays were made.#Correct. This explains the cause-and-effect relationship.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence is complete and clear?{
~A. Because students completed the forms.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=B. Students completed more forms after reminders were added.#Correct. This is a complete sentence.
~C. The findings about forms and.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~D. When the cafeteria.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG2_U04_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which checklist best matches Lesson 4 writing?{
=A. Clear topic sentence, logical structure, research evidence, transitions, and academic voice.#Correct. These are core expectations for expository writing from research findings.
~B. Personal opinion, random order, and no evidence.#Incorrect. This does not meet the standard.
~C. Copied source sentences only.#Incorrect. Expository writing should explain and organize information.
~D. Informal exaggeration and no structure.#Incorrect. That would weaken the writing.
}
